          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Who does guidance describe as a priority group for influenza vaccination?",
          "answer": "Current guidance in the retrieved material does not limit flu vaccination to a short list of priority groups. The main influenza guidance describes **routine annual vaccination for everyone aged 6 months and older who has no contraindication** (National immunization advisory committee and infectious diseases society guidance).\n\nThat same document lists the groups where **benefit is largest**:\n- Adults aged 65 and over\n- Children under 5, especially under 2\n- Pregnant people and those in the postpartum period (this also protects the infant in the first months of life)\n- Residents of long-term care\n- People with chronic lung, heart, kidney, liver, neurologic or metabolic disease\n- People with weakened immune systems\n- People with a body mass index in the obese range\n- Health-care workers, on transmission as well as personal grounds\n\nA general clinical reference on influenza also says severe illness is concentrated in older adults, young children, pregnant people and people with chronic conditions (Influenza, general clinical reference). Cardiology guidance on coronary artery disease and respiratory guidance on COPD both list flu vaccination as part of care for people with those conditions.\n\n**Where guidance differs:** The retrieved passages do not show a disagreement between authorities about which groups should be prioritized for vaccination. The disagreement section in the main influenza document concerns antiviral treatment in low-risk outpatients, not vaccine priority. I can't tell from these passages how strong the evidence is for each group, so I can't say how firmly each recommendation is held.\n\nIf you want to know whether this applies to you, a clinician or pharmacist can advise on your own situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
